Q: codeigniter update error: Invalid argument supplied for foreach() A: <p>The main reason you are not getting any results back from your update method is because you are not returning anything. </p>

<p>Secondly, I would change your <code>get_student_id()</code> method as it doesn't quite fit with MVC principals. It works for this particular task, however, you should really be getting the uri value in the controller and then passing it through to your model:</p>

<p><strong>Controller method</strong>:</p>

<pre><code>public function show_student($id)
{
    $data['single_student'] = $this-&gt;student_view_model-&gt;get_student_id($id);

    $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('view_student_update', $data);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Assuming that "show_student" would be the 2nd uri segment you can do the above.</p>

<p><strong>Model method</strong>:</p>

<pre><code>public function get_student_id($id)
{
    $query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get_where('tbl_student', array('student_id' =&gt; $id));

    return $query-&gt;row_array();
}
</code></pre>

<p>This way you can get the row for the student without depending on the <code>student_id</code> always being the 3rd uri segment.</p>

<p>So, with you're results method, you could:</p>

<p><strong>Controller Meothod</strong></p>

<pre><code>public function update_student()
{

    $data = array(
    'student_fname' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('first'),
    'student_lname' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('last'),
    'student_gender' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('gender'),
    'student_course' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('course'),
    'student_company' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('company')
    );

    $data['results'] = $this-&gt;student_view_model-&gt;update_student($this-&gt;input-&gt;post('hid'), $data);

    $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('view_student_list', $data);
}
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Model Method:</strong></p>

<pre><code>public function update_student($id, $data)
{

    $this-&gt;db-&gt;where('student_id', $id);

    $this-&gt;db-&gt;update('tbl_student', $data);

    return $this-&gt;get_student_id($id);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Again, with models you should always pass data to them (for quite a few reasons)!</p>

<p>Lastly, I would also think about change the name of <code>get_student_id()</code> to something like <code>get_student_by_id</code> or <code>get_student</code> as <code>get_student_id()</code> suggests t me that you are just going to be getting the id. Also, you really should look at using the <a href="https://ellislab.com/codeigniter/user-guide/libraries/form_validation.html" rel="nofollow">Form Validation</a> in codeigniter as your code is very, <strong>Very</strong> vulnerable at the minute.</p>

<p>Hope this helps! </p>

Q: Woocomerce no product found hook not working A: <p>Your theme is the reason, and it is visible in the code you posted. In the else branch, <code>cyberstore_mikado_woocommerce_content()</code> calls <code>wc_get_template( 'loop/no-products-found.php' )</code> directly. WooCommerce's own archive-product.php instead calls <code>do_action( 'woocommerce_no_products_found' )</code>, and it is that action which normally renders the template. Your theme skipped the action and went straight to the template, so nothing is hooked to fire and no priority or ordering change will help.</p>
<p>There is a second problem that would bite you even with a working hook. display_product_attribute_short_desc() reads global $product and then <code>get_the_terms( $product-&gt;get_id(), 'pa_merk' )</code>. On an empty attribute archive there is no product in the loop, so $product is null. You do not need it: on /merk/rosso/ the term you want is the queried object.</p>
<pre><code>    add_action( 'woocommerce_no_products_found', 'display_product_attribute_short_desc', 20 );
    function display_product_attribute_short_desc() {
        if ( ! is_tax( 'pa_merk' ) ) {
            return;
        }
        $term = get_queried_object();
        if ( $term instanceof WP_Term &amp;&amp; $term-&gt;description ) {
            echo wp_kses_post( wpautop( $term-&gt;description ) );
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>To make that hook fire again, pick one of these in a child theme. Cleanest: copy the theme's woocommerce.php into your child theme and replace the bare w<code>c_get_template( 'loop/no-products-found.php' )</code> call with <code>do_action( 'woocommerce_no_products_found' )</code>, which restores standard behaviour for every plugin that expects it, not just your snippet. Simplest: skip hooks entirely and put the term description into your own your-child-theme/woocommerce/loop/no-products-found.php, since <code>wc_get_template() </code> resolves the theme override first and the theme's direct call still goes through it.</p>
<p>Worth checking either way: because the theme replaced archive-product.php with its own woocommerce.php, woocommerce_archive_description may not run on these pages either, which is the hook that normally prints an attribute term's description on the first page of the archive. If that is also dead, restoring the standard template structure fixes both at once.</p>

Q: &quot;Undefined variable&quot; error emitted by CodeIgniter view A: <p>Try to add this expression in your controller </p>

<pre><code>$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('your_view', $data);
</code></pre>

<p>your code must like this : </p>

<pre><code>$id = $this-&gt;uri-&gt;segment(3);
$data['user'] = $this-&gt;authentication-&gt;show_students();
$data['single_user'] = $this-&gt;authentication-&gt;show_student_id($id);
$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('your_view', $data);
</code></pre>

<p>Hopefully it helps you.</p>

Q: Anchor tag inside span A: <ol>
<li><p>Add styles to the span, and also to the anchor inside the span:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#container span, #container span a {
    /* whatever */
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>You can't apply click events to a span tag without JavaScript. I suggest <a href="http://www.jquery.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jQuery</a> for ease:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>$('#container').on('click', 'span', function() {
    // JavaScript functionality here
});
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
